// TransferRingConfig is the geometry of SHARED_MEMORY_RING.
//
// # Why the parameters go over the control plane rather than only in the shared memory header
//
// Shared memory is **writable by both ends**. If the consumer read slot_count / slot_size from the header and
// computed offsets from them, a malicious or buggy producer could make the other side read out of bounds just by rewriting the header,
// in the other side's own address space, where nothing can stop it.
//
// So nervud sends the geometry in AttachTransferResult: it goes over the control plane, is produced
// by the kernel, and can't be tampered with by the peer. A copy also sits in the shared memory header, but that copy is **only for self-checks**
// (a mismatch means an implementation bug or trampled memory) and is never the basis for bounds calculations.
//
// # File descriptors
//
// When AttachTransferResult succeeds and mode == SHARED_MEMORY_RING, nervud attaches **exactly 2** fds with SCM_RIGHTS
// on the same data UDS, in a fixed order:
//
//	fd[0]  memfd    the ring memory. The producer gets PROT_READ|PROT_WRITE,
//	                the consumer gets PROT_READ (nervud guarantees this by means other than F_SEAL_WRITE:
//	                the consumer side is sent an fd duplicated O_RDONLY)
//	fd[1]  eventfd  the producer write(1)s after writing to wake the consumer; the consumer waits with read or poll
//
// Receiving a number of fds other than 2 must be treated as a protocol violation that closes the connection; don't just use the first few.
// A mismatched count means the two sides' understanding of the ABI has already diverged.
//
// # Memory layout (NVR1)
//
//	offset 0          header_bytes             first slot payload
//	┌───────────────┬────────────────────────┬─────────────────┐
//	│ ring header   │ slot descriptors       │ payload area    │
//	│ header_bytes  │ descriptor_bytes each  │ slot_size each  │
//	│               │ × slot_count           │ × slot_count    │
//	└───────────────┴────────────────────────┴─────────────────┘
//
// Total length = header_bytes + slot_count*descriptor_bytes + slot_count*slot_size,
// which must exactly equal the memfd's actual size; otherwise it's a protocol violation.
//
// Ring header (little-endian, matching the host byte order; this ABI is only used between processes on the same machine):
//
//	 0  magic[4]      "NVR1"
//	 4  version u32    currently 1
//	 8  slot_count u32     ┐
//	12  slot_size u32      ├ for self-checks only; bounds calculations must **not** use these three
//	16  descriptor_bytes u32 ┘
//	24  producer_cursor u64  written only by the producer, read-only for the consumer
//	32  consumer_cursor u64  written only by the consumer, read-only for the producer
//	40  reserved, must be 0
//
